>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Before anything else:</strong> bank requirements in Romania really do vary: between
      banks, between branches of the same bank, and over time. This guide tells you what's
      typical so you walk in prepared; it is not financial advice, and the bank's current
      official requirements always win. When in doubt, confirm with the branch before queueing.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>Do you need a Romanian account?</h2>
  <p>
    Not always. Card payments are near-universal in Cluj and many newcomers run on a home bank
    or a fintech card for months. A Romanian account starts to matter when you need to
    <strong>receive money locally</strong> (a salary, a scholarship, a deposit refund), pay
    recurring local bills, or when a landlord or employer simply expects a RON account (IBAN).
  </p>

  <h2>What banks typically ask for</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Passport or EU ID card</strong>, always.</li>
    <li><strong>Proof of why you're here</strong>, often requested: university enrollment
        certificate for students, a work contract for employees.</li>
    <li><strong>Romanian phone number</strong>, commonly needed for mobile banking activation
        (<a href="/cluj/guides/romanian-sim-card-foreigners/">get the SIM first</a>, it makes
        the bank visit smoother).</li>
    <li><strong>Address information</strong>: some banks record your local address; bring your
        rental contract or accommodation details just in case.</li>
    <li><strong>Residence documents / CNP</strong>: non-EU newcomers may be asked for a
        residence permit or the Romanian personal numeric code (CNP); practice varies, and EU
        citizens generally face lighter requirements. This is the area where branches differ
        most, so confirm before you go.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Students: ask about student accounts</h2>
  <p>
    Major banks offer student packages with reduced or zero monthly fees, typically against
    proof of enrollment. If you're at UBB, UMF, UTCN or USAMV, say so before the clerk opens a
    standard account; the difference over a few years is real money. More week-one wins like
    this live in our <a href="/cluj/student-support/">student support</a> section.
  </p>

  <h2>RON, EUR, and getting money in</h2>
  <p>
    Your main account will be in <strong>RON (lei)</strong>; that's what you'll pay rent and
    bills in. Most banks let you add a EUR (or USD) account alongside it, useful if family sends
    money from abroad. Ask how incoming international transfers are handled and what the
    exchange spread looks like; for money coming from home, a fintech transfer into your RON
    account is often cheaper than a classic bank wire. Compare before choosing a habit.
  </p>

  <h2>Cards, apps and fees</h2>
  <p>
    Expect a contactless debit card and a mobile app as standard; Romanian mobile banking is
    modern and most daily banking never touches a branch again. Before signing, ask for the fee
    sheet and check four numbers: monthly account fee, ATM withdrawals (own vs other banks),
    incoming transfers, and card replacement. The big retail banks in Cluj include
    <a href="https://www.bancatransilvania.ro" rel="noopener">Banca Transilvania</a> (headquartered
    here), <a href="https://www.bcr.ro" rel="noopener">BCR</a>,
    <a href="https://www.brd.ro" rel="noopener">BRD</a>,
    <a href="https://ing.ro" rel="noopener">ING</a>,
    <a href="https://www.raiffeisen.ro" rel="noopener">Raiffeisen</a> and
    <a href="https://www.unicredit.ro" rel="noopener">UniCredit</a>. Current conditions are on
    their official pages, and we deliberately don't rank them.
  </p>

  <h2>Why one branch says yes and another says no</h2>
  <p>
    A frustrating classic: one branch opens an account on your passport alone; another insists
    on a document you don't have yet. Internal policies, clerk discretion and how familiar the
    branch is with international customers all play a role. University-area and city-centre
    branches tend to have seen your situation before. If you're refused, it's often worth
    literally trying another branch of the same bank.
  </p>

  <h2>Questions to ask before you open</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>What's the monthly fee for my situation, and is there a student/newcomer package?</li>
    <li>Which documents do you need from <em>me specifically</em> (EU / non-EU, student / employee)?</li>
    <li>Is the mobile app available in English?</li>
    <li>What does an incoming EUR transfer cost, and how is it converted?</li>
    <li>Can I close the account easily if I leave Romania?</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Quick questions</h2>
  <h3>Can I open an account before arriving in Romania?</h3>
  <p>
    Generally no for classic banks. Expect an in-person visit with original documents. Plan it
    for your first weeks rather than before the flight.
  </p>
  <h3>Was I wrong to be asked for a document this guide doesn't mention?</h3>
  <p>
    No. Requirements shift and branches differ. If a request seems odd, it's fair to ask why
    it's needed, and reasonable to check another branch. If you're not sure whether something is
    normal, <a href="/cluj/paperwork-help/">ask us</a>. "Is this normal here?" is half of what
    we do.
  </p>
